          "from_string": "MRT Application Architecture Strategy (Single vs Per-Integration MRT Apps)",
          "to_string": "Spike: MRT Application Architecture Strategy (Single vs Per-Integration MRT Apps)"
        }
      ],
      "author": {
        "display_name": "Marcel Angelo Navarro",
        "name": "Marcel Angelo Navarro",
        "email": "marcel.a.n.navarro@accenture.com",
        "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/482e2e100517026f4980d6455214aa11?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FMN-6.png"
      },
      "created": "2026-07-24T18:26:41.922000+10:00"
    },
    {
      "items": [
        {
          "field": "description",
          "fieldtype": "jira",
          "to_string": "*Objective*\nProduce a TSD comparing two approaches for structuring the storefront's MRT (Managed Runtime) Node layer as integrations are built out — *Option A:* a single MRT application responsible for all integrations, vs *Option B:* a dedicated MRT application per integration/domain — so the squad makes a deliberate, signed-off decision on deployment topology before the integration build-out hardens it.\n\n*Background*\nThe storefront currently runs as a single PWA Kit application on MRT (the PWA Kit default), which today isolates upstream integrations only at the network level (per-origin {{undici}} connection pools). As integration work begins (~18 systems, nearly all not yet started), the number of MRT applications — and how integration/domain code is split across them — becomes a hard-to-retrofit decision affecting deploy/rollback, failure isolation, secrets, cost, and ownership. Tim has asked Angelo (FED) and Gideon (SFCC) to collaborate on this. Complements SFCC-122 (which inventoried what MRT can _consume_); this determines how many MRT apps we _deploy_ and how integration work is split across them. The as-is baseline is to be confirmed with Gideon / platform before the options analysis.\n\n*Tasks*\n\n* Confirm the current-state (as-is) baseline with Gideon/platform: single MRT app, proxies wired today, in-process isolation model, the cartridge vs MRT-mediated vs SFCC→MuleSoft integration boundary, deploy/secret model, and platform limits.\n* Confirm whether MRT supports multiple independent applications/projects in our org, and the cost model per environment/app.\n* Define and compare the options — Option A (single app), Option B (per-integration/domain app), and any viable middle option (single modular app) — across deploy/rollback, failure isolation (event loop / memory / process, not just sockets), secrets & security blast radius, performance/LCP, cost, and ownership.\n* Assess the MRT execution/concurrency model and its effect on failure blast radius.\n* Recommend an option with rationale tied to Zimmermann constraints (LCP ≤ 2s, fail-independently, multi-region, squad ownership).\n* Capture the decision in a TSD using the team template; secure Technical Lead sign-off.\n\n*Definition of Done*\nA TSD comparing Option A vs Option B (and any middle option), with an agreed as-is baseline, a recommended option and rationale, and Technical Lead sign-off — sufficient to set the MRT application topology before integration build-out begins."
        }
      ],
      "author": {
        "display_name": "Marcel Angelo Navarro",
        "name": "Marcel Angelo Navarro",
        "email": "marcel.a.n.navarro@accenture.com",
        "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/482e2e100517026f4980d6455214aa11?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FMN-6.png"
      },
      "created": "2026-07-24T18:25:38.947000+10:00"
